# Project: Schema

Purpose:
  Define the core project hierarchy: Project > Domain > Module > Feature > Component.
  This is the SHAPE of the hierarchy — concrete instances live in sibling files.

Hierarchy:
  Project:
    description: Top-level software system

  Domain:
    description: Coherent business capability
    schema: ./domains.sdd

  Module:
    description: Technical or functional boundary inside a domain
    schema: ./modules.sdd

  Feature:
    description: Executable product behavior inside a module
    schema: ./features.sdd

  Component:
    description: Implementation boundary inside a feature
    schema: ./components.sdd

ResolutionOrder:
  PROJECT > DOMAIN > MODULE > FEATURE > COMPONENT > CODE

Rules:
  [PS1] Every project domain MUST have a model file (bax ./domains.sdd).
  [PS2] Every domain SHOULD expose its boundaries and declare owned components.
  [PS3] Project elements MUST NOT duplicate information owned by global SDD systems — reference them instead.
  [PS4] Feature declarations MUST reference the chain type they run under (bax chains/chains.sdd).
  [PS5] Feature/component declarations MUST reference task and decision rules (bax tasks/tasks.sdd, decisions/workflow.sdd).
  [PS6] Component code paths MUST be mapped to project ownership.
  [PS7] AI SHOULD use this project model to locate code before searching the source tree.
  [PS8] This file MUST NOT redeclare concrete domain/module lists already owned by domains.sdd/modules.sdd.
  [PS9] Populating hierarchy layers beyond what current analysis justifies is FORBIDDEN (bax ./instantiation.sdd).
  [PS10] Every project element (domain, module, feature, component) MUST have one clear owner.

Navigation:
  Domains: @domains.sdd
  Modules: @modules.sdd
  Features: @features.sdd
  Components: @components.sdd
  Map: @map.sdd
  Indexes: @indexes.sdd
  Architecture: @architecture.sdd
  Flows: @flows.sdd
  Dependencies: @dependencies.sdd
  Instantiation: @instantiation.sdd

State: +
